<UserControl x:Class="D47.Render.CapabilityView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:local="clr-namespace:D47.Render"
             Background="{x:Static local:Palette.Background}"
             AutomationProperties.Name="Capability answer"
             Padding="32"
             FontFamily="pack://application:,,,/D47.Render;component/assets/fonts/#Saira"
             FontStretch="Condensed">

    <!--
        VR-legible density from the first commit: large type, high contrast, few
        rows. The overlays present this same render, and a target tuned for a
        flat monitor is unreadable in a headset.

        The colors and the typeface are both set here, once, because they are
        properties of what is drawn rather than of any one surface: the panel and
        both overlays present this render and so all get them together. The
        colors are Elite Dangerous' own and live in Palette.cs.

        Saira Condensed, embedded — see D47.Render.csproj. Body text is Regular
        400, which is the inherited default; anything emphatic asks for
        FontWeight="SemiBold" and gets the 600 face.

        The family is #Saira and the width is a FontStretch, which is not a
        stylistic choice. Saira treats width as an axis: both files declare the
        typographic family "Saira" at stretch Condensed, and "Saira Condensed" is
        only the Win32 name of the Regular file. Naming that instead resolves —
        and then answers every weight with the 400 file, because a Win32 family
        holds one face. So SemiBold would silently come back synthetically
        emboldened from Regular. If the width ever moves to Saira Semi Condensed,
        the fallback the issue records, this attribute is the edit.

        The trailing slash on the folder and the #Family suffix are both
        required, and a URI missing either does not fail: WPF falls back to the
        system font and draws. TypefaceTests resolves the face rather than
        reading the string back, for exactly that reason. PaletteTests resolves
        the colors off the built tree for the same reason: a brush that never
        resolved is null, and a null brush paints nothing without complaining.
    -->
    <UserControl.Resources>
        <local:DisplayModelTemplateSelector x:Key="ByDisplayModel" />

        <!--
            WPF's own, which is why there is no converter class in this project.
            Named for the question it answers rather than for what it converts,
            because the call site reads as a sentence and "BooleanToVisibility"
            does not.
        -->
        <BooleanToVisibilityConverter x:Key="WhenThereIsSomethingToSay" />

        <DataTemplate x:Key="ListDisplay">
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Result.Items}"
                          Focusable="False">
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate>
                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding}"
                                   FontSize="30"
                                   Foreground="{x:Static local:Palette.BodyText}"
                                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   Margin="0,0,0,16" />
                    </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
        </DataTemplate>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!--
        AutomationProperties.Name is not decoration, and it is on the UserControl
        above rather than here. Without it, a control holding a non-visual object
        announces that object's ToString() as its accessible name — the whole
        Answer record, type names and backing-field spellings included — which a
        screen reader reads aloud and automation cannot match on.

        It was on this ContentControl until #167, where it did nothing at all:
        ContentControl creates no automation peer, so a name set here reaches
        nothing, while reading the property straight back off the element says it
        is set. The UserControl is the element with a peer, and it is also the
        element every surface hosts — so it is the thing that has a name to give.
        CapabilityViewTests asks the peers rather than the elements, which is
        the only version of the question that can fail this way.
    -->
    <StackPanel>

        <!--
            Above the answer, and only when there is one. The answer is what was
            asked for and this is not, so it goes where it is read first and then
            got out of the way of.

            Collapsed rather than hidden when there is nothing to say, so it
            takes no room at all. The game overlay and the headset are both sized
            to what the render wants, and a blank line held open for a notice
            nobody has would cost every surface the space forever.

            The wording lives here rather than arriving finished from a caller.
            What the notice says is the render's to word, and prose composed
            outside it would be a second place for the panel and the headset to
            disagree about what it says.
        -->
        <StackPanel Visibility="{Binding HasNotice,
                                Converter={StaticResource WhenThereIsSomethingToSay}}">

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding UpdateWaiting, StringFormat=Update available — {0}}"
                       FontSize="24"
                       Foreground="{x:Static local:Palette.BodyText}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" />

            <Border BorderBrush="{x:Static local:Palette.BodyText}"
                    BorderThickness="0,1,0,0"
                    Opacity="0.35"
                    Margin="0,12,0,24" />

        </StackPanel>

        <ContentControl Content="{Binding Answer}"
                        ContentTemplateSelector="{StaticResource ByDisplayModel}" />

    </StackPanel>

</UserControl>
